```\
/goal Build Project Corkboard: a local-only project dashboard I run from the terminal on this Mac mini.\
\
REPO / NAME\
- Product name: Project Corkboard\
- Directory / repo folder: dev-project-corkboard\
- Create and work only inside that folder. Do not scatter files elsewhere.\
\
HOW TO WORK\
- Use multiple agents in parallel whenever it will finish v1 faster. Split work across agents instead of doing everything serially.\
- Sensible split examples (adjust if you have a better cut):\
  - Agent A: repo scaffold, server, config schema, README, run/stop commands\
  - Agent B: corkboard UI (HTML/CSS/JS), cards, colors, sample data, empty states\
  - Agent C: filesystem scanner, git/manifest detection, ignore rules, scan review flow\
- Keep a single coherent repo. Agents must not invent conflicting stacks, ports, or config formats. One owner for the contract (config shape + API/routes + file layout); others implement against it.\
- Merge into one working app before calling v1 done. No half-integrated branches left behind.\
- Prefer speed with a working board over perfect architecture theater.\
\
CONTEXT\
I have too many open projects on this machine and on external drives. I lose track of what each one is. I need a glanceable localhost page: corkboard / post-it wall with one card per project and just enough info to remember it. v1 must include a SCAN feature: I give it a path on the internal disk or an external volume and it finds likely ongoing projects. After v1 I will add drag/drop, richer notes, open-in-editor, etc. You may add tasteful design and small features that help a solo local-first developer. Do not build a SaaS, auth, cloud sync, analytics, or a heavy framework app.\
\
PRODUCT (v1)\
- Local webpage on localhost (pick a port, document it).\
- Visual metaphor: corkboard with pushpins and cards/post-its. Card color settable from config (picker can wait).\
- One card per project. Minimum fields:\
  - name\
  - one-line summary\
  - status (active | paused | idea | shipped | archive \'97 refine if needed)\
  - optional color, tags, disk path\
  - last activity if cheap (folder mtime and/or last git commit)\
- Empty / first-run state must explain how to scan and how to add a project.\
- Desktop-first, readable in a laptop browser.\
- Branding in the UI and README: \'93Project Corkboard\'94.\
\
SCAN FEATURE (required, not optional)\
I must be able to point the app at a directory and have it discover possible projects.\
\
Input\
- Path I provide: any folder on the Mac or a mounted external drive.\
- Configurable default roots in config (e.g. ~/Projects plus a placeholder for /Volumes/Whatever).\
- UI and/or CLI: \'93scan this path\'94 with depth limit.\
\
What counts as a project\
Use evidence. A random folder is not a project.\
Strong signal:\
- Directory is a git working tree root (.git exists here, not only in a child like node_modules).\
Supporting signals (increase confidence):\
- README / README.md\
- Manifests: package.json, pnpm-workspace.yaml, pyproject.toml, requirements.txt, Cargo.toml, go.mod, Gemfile, composer.json, mix.exs, CMakeLists.txt, Makefile, *.xcodeproj, *.xcworkspace, Package.swift, pubspec.yaml, etc.\
- Source-ish layout (src/, app/, lib/, cmd/) without being a dependency dump\
Git enrichment when .git exists (read-only):\
- last commit date + subject (one line)\
- current branch\
- whether worktree is dirty (optional if cheap)\
- remotes (optional)\
Ranking:\
- Prefer git roots with recent commits or a README as \'93likely ongoing\'94.\
- Folders with only loose files and no git/manifest = ignore or mark low-confidence, do not auto-pin.\
\
Safety / performance\
- Do not modify anything inside scanned trees. This app is read-only toward my projects.\
- Do not recurse from / or ~ by default. Only the path I give (plus configured roots).\
- Skip and do not descend: node_modules, .git, .venv, venv, __pycache__, dist, build, target, .next, .cache, Library, .Trash, system volumes, and similar junk. Make the skip list easy to edit.\
- Max depth configurable (default something like 3\'964 under the given root).\
- External drives: if the path is missing/unmounted, fail soft with a clear message. Don\'92t hang the UI. Time out slow stats.\
- Don\'92t follow symlinks into loops.\
- Cap results (e.g. first N hits) so a huge disk doesn\'92t freeze the page.\
- Never treat dev-project-corkboard itself as a mysterious third-party project if it appears in a scan; it may show up as this app.\
\
Scan UX (v1)\
- Trigger scan from the board (path field + button) and/or a CLI command documented in README.\
- Show results as a review list: path, detected name, confidence, last git commit if any, \'93add to board\'94 / \'93ignore\'94.\
- Adding saves into the app\'92s config (projects.json or yaml). Re-scan should not duplicate already-tracked paths.\
- Demo mode: include a small sample tree OR sample config cards so the board looks alive without scanning my real disk first.\
- Never invent what my real projects are about. One-liner may come from README title/first paragraph or last commit subject; otherwise use folder name + \'93scanned project\'94.\
\
DATA\
- Source of truth: config file inside dev-project-corkboard that I can edit by hand.\
- Scan writes candidates I accept into that config.\
- Ship 4\'968 clearly marked SAMPLE cards so first paint isn\'92t empty.\
- Document how to set scan roots, ignore rules, and replace samples.\
\
STACK\
- Start from terminal with one documented command from inside dev-project-corkboard.\
- HTML + CSS + vanilla JS for the UI.\
- Tiny local server (Python preferred on macOS unless you have a leaner Node path). Fewest moving parts. No Docker required.\
- No required cloud fonts/CDNs; must work offline once started.\
- Minimal dependencies, versions pinned if any.\
\
UX / DESIGN\
- Cork texture, pins, slight rotation, paper/post-it look, still readable.\
- Status and last-activity visible at a glance.\
- Card click expands path, git snippet, tags \'97 stay on the board.\
- Header: \'93Project Corkboard\'94, count, scan control, simple status filter if cheap.\
\
ENGINEERING\
- All project files live in dev-project-corkboard.\
- Clean layout. README: what Project Corkboard is, how to run, stop, scan a path, add/ignore a project, change roots, ignore list, external drive notes.\
- Filesystem errors are soft failures.\
- Don\'92t touch my other repos except reading metadata.\
\
ACCEPTANCE (v1 done when)\
- I can enter dev-project-corkboard, start the server from terminal, open localhost, and see Project Corkboard with sample cards.\
- I can enter a path (or CLI scan) and see candidate projects based on git/manifests, not every subdirectory.\
- I can add a candidate to the board; refresh persists it.\
- Unmounted / bad path does not crash the server.\
- Colors + statuses render.\
- README is enough for future-me.\
- Next /goal can add: persist pin positions, color picker, open in Finder/VS Code/Cursor, live git status, search.\
\
OUT OF SCOPE\
- Auth, sync, Electron, full kanban, rewriting my other projects, scanning the entire home directory unbidden.\
\
PROCESS\
Create the dev-project-corkboard directory. Spin parallel agents immediately after the shared contract (layout, config schema, port, scan API) is written. Scaffold a working board and the scanner in parallel, then wire scan \uc0\u8594  review \u8594  config \u8594  cards. When v1 meets acceptance, say so and list: run command, config path, scan command/UI, which work was parallelized, and the 3 best next features.\
